public class Solution {
    public int maxUniqueSplit(String s) {
        int result = 0;
        backtrack(s, 0, new HashSet<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private void backtrack(String s, int start, Set<String> set, int[] result) {
        if (start == s.length()) {
            result[0] = Math.max(result[0], set.size());
            return;
        }

        for (int i = start + 1; i <= s.length(); i++) {
            String substring = s.substring(start, i);
            if (!set.contains(substring)) {
                set.add(substring);
                backtrack(s, i, set, result);
                set.remove(substring);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code defines a class `Solution` with a method `maxUniqueSplit` that takes a string `s` and returns the maximum number of unique substrings. It uses a backtracking approach to explore all possible splits of the string and keep track of unique substrings in a set. The best result is updated during each traversal.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxUniqueSplit(String s) {
        return backtrack(s, 0, new HashSet<String>());
    }

    private int backtrack(String s, int index, Set<String> seen) {
        if (index == s.length()) {
            return seen.size();
        }
        int maxUnique = 0;
        for (int i = index + 1; i <= s.length(); i++) {
            String substring = s.substring(index, i);
            if (!seen.contains(substring)) {
                seen.add(substring);
                maxUnique = Math.max(maxUnique, backtrack(s, i, seen));
                seen.remove(substring);
            }
        }
        return maxUnique;
    }
}
