This function works by first identifying the smallest digit (`i`) that is smaller than the digit next to it (from the right). If no such digit exists, it means the digits are in descending order, and thus rearranging them will not result in a larger number, so we return -1. If such a digit (`i`) is found, we then find the smallest digit (`j`) greater than `digits[i]` to the right of `i`. These two digits are swapped. Finally, the digits to the right of `i` are sorted in ascending order to get the smallest possible number greater than `n`.

```java
public static int findSmallestInteger(int n) {
    char[] digits = Integer.toString(n).toCharArray();
    int length = digits.length;

    // Find the first digit that can be swapped to get a larger number
    int i;
    for (i = length - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
        if (digits[i] < digits[i + 1]) {
            break;
        }
    }

    // If no such digit is found, return -1
    if (i == -1) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Find the smallest digit on the right side of (i) but larger than digits[i]
    int j;
    for (j = length - 1; j > i; j--) {
        if (digits[j] > digits[i]) {
            break;
        }
    }

    // Swap digits[i] with the found digit
    char temp = digits[i];
    digits[i] = digits[j];
    digits[j] = temp;

    // Sort the digits after (i) in ascending order
    Arrays.sort(digits, i + 1, length);

    // Convert the character array back to an integer
    long val = Long.parseLong(new String(digits));

    // Check if the number is greater than n and within the integer range
    return (val > n && val <= Integer.MAX_VALUE) ? (int) val : -1;
}
